<div class="bg-amber-50 border-l-4 border-amber-500 rounded-r-xl p-6 mb-8">
<p class="text-[11px] font-black text-brandOrange uppercase tracking-widest mb-2">KEY TAKEAWAYS</p>
<p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed text-sm mb-4 speakable">Three mistakes cost OEM importers more money than any other factor in 2026: misclassifying HS codes, quoting from outdated tariff formulas, and leaving IEEPA refunds unclaimed. This article walks through each mistake with real dollar figures, then shows the correct landed cost formula you should be using instead.</p>
<ul class="text-sm text-slate-700 space-y-2 list-disc pl-5">
<li><strong>Mistake 1 — Wrong HS code:</strong> Classifying a power bank under charger code 8504.40 instead of 8507.60 underreports duty by 3.4 percentage points. On a $50,000 shipment, that's $1,700 in underpaid duty — which CBP can audit retroactively for 5 years. Always demand a signed HS classification certificate with the full 10-digit HTSUS subheading.</li>
<li><strong>Mistake 2 — Using 2024 tariff formulas:</strong> The "2× FOB" rule of thumb broke in 2026. Section 301 (25%) plus the tiered forced-labor tariff (10-12.5%) stack additively on FOB value. A typical 5,000-unit GaN charger order now lands at 1.35-1.45× FOB, not 2×. Quoting from the old formula either overprices your bid or destroys your margin.</li>
<li><strong>Mistake 3 — Leaving IEEPA refunds unclaimed:</strong> If you paid the 54% reciprocal tariff between April 2025 and February 2026, those duties are recoverable. Unliquidated entries can be corrected via Post Summary Correction. The refund window closes 180 days after liquidation — for entries from early 2025, that deadline is approaching fast.</li>
</ul>
