 <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">2. HS Code Mistakes That Cost Importers 5-15% in Overpaid Duty</h2>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">Every customs entry starts with a 6-to-10-digit code. Get it wrong, and you overpay by 5-15% on every unit — or worse, underpay and get flagged for audit. In 2026, with Section 301 at 25% and the forced-labor tariff adding 10-12.5%, the gap between the right code and the wrong one has never been wider. The 3 traps below are the most common misclassification errors we see in OEM charger and power bank shipments. Each one has a specific CBP ruling you can cite to keep your entry clean.</p>

 <!-- Snippet-Optimized HS Code Quick Reference -->
 <div class="bg-gradient-to-r from-amber-50 to-white rounded-xl p-6 border-2 border-amber-300 mb-6">
 <p class="text-[11px] font-black text-amber-700 uppercase tracking-widest mb-4">HS Code Quick Reference — What Google Doesn't Show</p>

 <h3 class="text-lg font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">What Is the HS Code for Power Bank?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-700 text-sm mb-4 speakable"><strong>Power banks use HS 8507.60 (lithium-ion accumulators).</strong> But the 6-digit code is only the starting point. The US 10-digit HTSUS subheading — 8507.60.0020 — determines your actual duty rate: 3.4% MFN + 25% Section 301 + 10-12.5% forced-labor tariff = ~38.4-40.9% total. EU importers use CN 8507 60 00 with 0% duty + national VAT (19-27%). Misclassification between 8507.60 (power bank) and 8504.40 (charger) is the #1 customs audit trigger for electronics importers, the duty delta is ~3.4 percentage points. <strong>Always</strong> request a signed HS classification certificate from your factory before your first shipment.</p>

