<!-- Section 12: FAQs -->
<section id="faq" class="mb-16">
 <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
 <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">13. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
 <div class="space-y-4">
 <div class="bg-white rounded-lg p-5 border border-slate-200">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">What is the current import duty on chargers from China in 2026?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer">GaN and wall chargers (HS 8504.40) face approximately <strong>35-37.5% combined US duty</strong> in August 2026: 0% MFN base + 25% Section 301 + 10-12.5% Section 301 forced-labor tariff (tiered by economy; in force since July 24, 2026, when it replaced the expired Section 122 surcharge). Power banks (HS 8507.60) face <strong>~38.4-40.9%</strong> (3.4% MFN + 25% Section 301 + 10-12.5% forced-labor tariff). Plus MPF 0.3464%, HMF 0.125%, and customs broker fees. EU duty rates remain 0-3.7% MFN + VAT 19-27%.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-lg p-5 border border-slate-200">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">What happens if I use the wrong HS code for my charger shipment?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer">Using the wrong HS code triggers 3 costly consequences: (1) overpaid duty of 5-15% on every unit — on a $50,000 shipment, that's $2,500-7,500 in unnecessary cost, (2) CBP can flag your entry for audit, retroactively applying the correct rate plus interest for up to 5 years of past entries, and (3) customs holds delay delivery by 2-6 weeks while your broker files corrections. Always request a signed HS classification certificate from your factory referencing the current HTSUS subheading and the latest CBP cross-ruling.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-lg p-5 border border-slate-200">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">What is the difference between FOB and DDP shipping for chargers?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer">FOB (Free on Board) means the supplier covers costs until goods are loaded onto the vessel at Shenzhen port; you arrange and pay for international freight, insurance, import duties, and destination clearance. DDP (Delivered Duty Paid) means the supplier handles everything door-to-door, including duties and customs clearance. DDP typically costs 15-30% more than FOB but eliminates customs paperwork and surprise charges. WOWOHCOOL offers both FOB and DDP shipping options.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-lg p-5 border border-slate-200">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">What documents are required to import chargers from China to the US?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer">Standard documents include commercial invoice, packing list, bill of lading or air waybill, certificate of origin, and HS classification certificate. Power banks additionally require MSDS (Material Safety Data Sheet) and UN38.3 test summary for lithium battery shipping. ISF filing is required 24 hours before vessel departure for ocean freight. WOWOHCOOL provides complete documentation packages with every OEM order, including CE, FCC, RoHS, and UL certification support.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-lg p-5 border border-slate-200">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">How do I calculate total landed cost for charger imports from China?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer">Total landed cost = FOB price + ocean or air freight + insurance + Section 301 duty + forced-labor tariff duty + MFN duty + MPF (0.3464%) + HMF (0.125%) + ISF filing + customs broker fees + drayage + 3PL receiving. For a typical $10 FOB charger in 5,000-unit lots, expect approximately $13.50-14.75 landed at a US warehouse, a 35-45% premium over FOB. <strong><a href="/contact/" class="text-brandOrange hover:underline">Request a landed cost quote with current tariff calculations for your specific product.</a></strong></p>
 </div>
 </div>
 </div>
</section>

